\section{Read \code{complete} before quoting a share}{

A sparse matrix rejects most shuffles, so the ensemble can come back shorter
than requested; see \code{\link[=surrogate_ensemble]{surrogate_ensemble()}}. A \code{share_ge} computed over seven
draws and one computed over two hundred are not the same claim, and \code{B} alone
does not distinguish them from what the caller asked for.
}

\section{Reading \code{share_ge}}{

It is a position, not a p-value. \code{share_ge = 0} means the observation exceeds
every draw; \code{share_ge = 1} means every draw exceeds it. Values near 0.5 mean
the shuffle reproduces the observation, so that diagnostic says more about
how the ratings were distributed than about how the factors were connected.
}

\section{\code{type_share} is weak evidence for a common type}{

Nearly 70\% of the reference corpus is diffuse-amplified, so a shuffled matrix
usually lands there too. A \code{type_share} near 1 for a common type therefore
means very little -- it says the null also produces that type, not that the
observation is unremarkable. The per-metric positions are the informative
part. \code{type_share} earns its keep when the observed type is an uncommon one,
where a high value is a genuine warning that the rating distribution alone
would have put the system in that corner.
}

